{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response items && \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" validate --help && \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json, os, hashlib\npaths={k:os.environ[k] for k in ['STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST','STAMPED_OPERATIONAL_MANIFEST','STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT']}\nfor k,p in paths.items():\n d=json.load(open(p)); print(k, 'sha256',hashlib.sha256(open(p,'rb').read()).hexdigest())\n if k=='STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST': print('capture keys',list(d), 'head',d.get('head'), 'target_commit',d.get('target_commit'), 'entries',len(d.get('entries',[])))\n if k=='STAMPED_OPERATIONAL_MANIFEST': print('operational fields',list(d), 'same capture',d.get('capture_sha256'))\n if k=='STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT': print('receipt keys',list(d), 'worker',d.get('worker')); print('actions',d.get('actions')); print('artifacts',[(x.get('path'),x.get('kind'),x.get('sha256')) for x in d.get('artifacts',[])])\nPY\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report/report.md\" && nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/README.md\" && nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/protocol.md\" && cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/config\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response items && \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" validate --help && \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json, os, hashlib\npaths={k:os.environ[k] for k in ['STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST','STAMPED_OPERATIONAL_MANIFEST','STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT']}\nfor k,p in paths.items():\n d=json.load(open(p)); print(k, 'sha256',hashlib.sha256(open(p,'rb').read()).hexdigest())\n if k=='STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST': print('capture keys',list(d), 'head',d.get('head'), 'target_commit',d.get('target_commit'), 'entries',len(d.get('entries',[])))\n if k=='STAMPED_OPERATIONAL_MANIFEST': print('operational fields',list(d), 'same capture',d.get('capture_sha256'))\n if k=='STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT': print('receipt keys',list(d), 'worker',d.get('worker')); print('actions',d.get('actions')); print('artifacts',[(x.get('path'),x.get('kind'),x.get('sha256')) for x in d.get('artifacts',[])])\nPY\nnl -ba \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report/report.md\" && nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/README.md\" && nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/protocol.md\" && cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/config\"'","aggregated_output":"✔ The default environment has been installed.\nstamped-checklist:must/001\tmust\tS.1\tAre all files and directories nested under a common root?\nstamped-checklist:must/002\tmust\tS.1\tAre datasets included, linked, or referenced reachable from the code and environment specifications without crossing the boundary of a common root?\nstamped-checklist:must/003\tmust\tS.1\tIs external software included in the environment or linked as submodules within the project boundary?\nstamped-checklist:must/004\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre version control systems such as `Git` used for code, text, documentation, and configuration files?\nstamped-checklist:must/005\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre version control systems such as `git-annex`, DataLad, or `Git` LFS used for large binary data?\nstamped-checklist:must/006\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre the exact environment specifications used to generate a set of results included in the provenance records to link computational actions to particular environments?\nstamped-checklist:must/007\tmust\tA.1\tIs a `README.md` or `Makefile` included with instructions for installation and usage?\nstamped-checklist:must/008\tmust\tA.1\tIs there a clear starting point for users to start reproducing results (e.g., a main script, a workflow definition, or a container image)?\nstamped-checklist:must/009\tmust\tP.1\tAre relative paths used in scripts, avoiding hardcoded or system-specific paths like `C:\\Users\\...` or `/home/user/...`?\nstamped-checklist:must/010\tmust\tP.1\tAre all software dependencies included in the environment specification, rather than relying on pre-installed tools?\nstamped-checklist:must/011\tmust\tP.1\tHave all assumptions about the host system's configuration been documented (e.g., specific OS versions, required system libraries, or environment variables)?\nstamped-checklist:must/012\tmust\tP.2\tIs there a clear list of system requirements and dependencies documented in the README or environment specifications?\nstamped-checklist:must/013\tmust\tP.3\tAre environment specifications (e.g., Dockerfiles, `pyproject.toml`, `package.json`) included in the version control system alongside code and data?\nstamped-checklist:must/014\tmust\tP.3\tIs there a process for updating environment specifications when dependencies change, and are these updates tracked in version control?\nstamped-checklist:must/015\tmust\tD.1\tAre environment specifications (e.g., container digests, frozen package manifests) included to ensure others can attempt to exactly replicate the environment?\nstamped-checklist:must/016\tmust\tD.1\tAre environment specifications shared in a way that others can access and use them (e.g., published container images, archived environment files)?\nstamped-checklist:must/017\tmust\tD.1\tIs there documentation on how to obtain and use the environment specifications for reproduction?\nstamped-checklist:should/001\tshould\tT.2\tIs a common version control system (e.g., `Git`, DVC, `git-annex`, or DataLad) used across all components?\nstamped-checklist:should/002\tshould\tA.2\tIs the workflow tested regularly to ensure instructions remain accurate?\nstamped-checklist:should/003\tshould\tM.1\tAre raw data, processed data, code, and environment definitions separated into distinct modules?\nstamped-checklist:should/009\tshould\tM.4\tAre restricted data or software kept in their own modules?\nstamped-checklist:should/004\tshould\tE.1\tIs the pipeline tested in a fresh container, batch job, clean virtual machine, or cloud-based instance?\nstamped-checklist:should/005\tshould\tE.1\tDoes the workflow spawn disposable environments for each execution, allowing for isolation of runs?\nstamped-checklist:should/006\tshould\tD.2\tAre the exact environment specifications used to generate a specific set of results regularly tested to ensure they can be reconstituted as intended?\nstamped-checklist:should/007\tshould\tD.2\tAre environment artifacts archived within the research object where possible (e.g., executable binaries, container images)?\nstamped-checklist:should/008\tshould\tD.2\tIs there a process for updating environment specifications when dependencies change, and are these updates tracked in version control?\nstamped-checklist:may/001\tmay\tM.2\tAre external datasets or software dependencies included as submodules or linked as submodules?\nstamped-checklist:may/002\tmay\tM.2\tIs the modular structure documented to clarify how components relate and can be recombined?\nstamped-checklist:may/003\tmay\tM.2\tAre modular boundaries defined in a way that allows independent updates without breaking the overall workflow?\nstamped-checklist:may/004\tmay\tM.2\tIs there a clear mechanism for composing modules together (e.g., `git` submodules, or container orchestration)?\nstamped-checklist:may/005\tmay\tM.2\tIs there documentation or tooling to support users in understanding and navigating the modular structure?\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py validate [-h] response\n\npositional arguments:\n  response\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help  show this help message and exit\nSTAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST sha256 4cb6043b8fa2409c631a8c49a0b0b5ea0feec21e0162f4e924f9c08d1be8939f\ncapture keys ['capture_finished_at', 'capture_status', 'captured_at', 'declared_artifacts', 'entries', 'errors', 'git_dependencies', 'git_metadata', 'head_commit', 'kind', 'omissions', 'policy', 'schema', 'workspace'] head None target_commit None entries 1162\nSTAMPED_OPERATIONAL_MANIFEST sha256 4cb6043b8fa2409c631a8c49a0b0b5ea0feec21e0162f4e924f9c08d1be8939f\noperational fields ['capture_finished_at', 'capture_status', 'captured_at', 'declared_artifacts', 'entries', 'errors', 'git_dependencies', 'git_metadata', 'head_commit', 'kind', 'omissions', 'policy', 'schema', 'workspace'] same capture None\nSTAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT sha256 b989c63ef99528772a7594ef4adba6eb1e961ef6c9c087485740c28468bb9d06\nreceipt keys ['capture', 'capture_finished_at', 'capture_status', 'captured_at', 'errors', 'head_commit', 'kind', 'omissions', 'schema_version', 'seed_identities', 'worker'] worker {'pid': 59612, 'returncode': 0, 'stop_reason': 'exited', 'stopped_at': '2026-10-16T06:53:47.574212+00:00', 'timed_out': False}\nactions None\nartifacts []\n     1\t# Potential gains and brain activity\n     2\t\n     3\t## Result\n     4\t\n     5\t**The supplied group maps do not support a greater gain-related fMRI response in vmPFC after whole-brain correction.** Among 54 equal-indifference participants, a positive one-sample group effect appeared at 24 voxels in three small contiguous clusters; the strongest voxel was at MNI (−26, −102, −3.6) mm (t(53)=6.62; max-statistic permutation FWE p=0.00060). These clusters were in the far posterior/inferior brain, not vmPFC. In the prespecified vmPFC coordinate locator, no voxel survived whole-brain correction. Its largest uncorrected t was 3.92 at (−2, 26, −18) mm, with whole-brain FWE p=0.932.\n     6\t\n     7\tThe mean cope averaged over the vmPFC locator was −0.0075 normalized-regressor units (95% t interval −0.031 to 0.016). This interval includes zero. It is a descriptive regional summary; the inferential decision uses the whole-brain max-statistic correction. The positive posterior clusters do not establish the vmPFC hypothesis.\n     8\t\n     9\t![Whole-brain group t map and participant estimates](../figures/group_gain_effect.png)\n    10\t\n    11\t*The left and middle panels show unthresholded t statistics in MNI slices. Black outlines mark voxels that survive positive whole-brain max-T FWE p<.05. The right panel shows individual participant estimates for the mean vmPFC locator and its selected peak voxel; the peak-voxel distribution is post-hoc descriptive.*\n    12\t\n    13\t## Analysis\n    14\t\n    15\tThe analysis includes all 54 maps listed in the supplied manifest and all 54 metadata rows; there were no exclusions. Map IDs matched one-to-one, all SHA-256 digests matched `input/sources.json`, and all maps had identical 97×115×81 grids, 2×2×2.4 mm voxel sizes, and affines. All voxel values were finite. Inference used the 198,029 voxels with finite, nonzero values in every participant map. The intersection mask was inspected and is provided as `results/common_coverage_mask.nii.gz`; p maps use NaN outside this mask.\n    16\t\n    17\tAt each covered voxel we estimated the equal-weight mean of the participant cope maps and its one-sample t statistic against zero. The directional alternative was positive. Whole-brain family-wise error was controlled by the maximum positive t statistic over all covered voxels in 10,000 random participant sign flips (seed 20261016; plus-one p-value correction). The 95th percentile of the null maximum-t distribution was 5.38. The original NARPS instructions require a whole-brain-corrected yes/no decision, which motivates correction over the complete brain rather than testing vmPFC alone ([NARPS analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)).\n    18\t\n    19\tThe vmPFC locator was defined before inference as |MNI x|≤10 mm, y≥25 mm, z≤15 mm, restricted to common coverage. It serves only to describe localization; the p-values remain whole-brain corrected. This broad coordinate rule is not an atlas segmentation and should not be read as precise cytoarchitectonic boundaries. A manual SciPy one-sample t calculation at the strongest whole-brain voxel matched the vectorized statistic to numerical precision.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Interpretation and limits\n    22\t\n    23\tA lack of corrected evidence in vmPFC means this analysis did not detect a positive group mean effect there at the study’s whole-brain-corrected threshold. It does not demonstrate that the true effect is zero, rule out smaller effects, establish equivalence, or imply that no individual showed a positive effect. The uncorrected vmPFC peak is not evidence for the hypothesis after accounting for the whole-brain search.\n    24\t\n    25\tThe supplied values are run-averaged participant contrast parameter estimates from the AO86 gain model, not standardized t/z statistics or a group result. In this task, the potential-gain range was twice the loss range, but the upstream gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution, and gain and loss effects were modeled separately. Thus cope units are normalized-regressor units, not change in response per dollar, and this group test does not compare gain sensitivity with loss sensitivity. Since participant variance maps and the full executable first-level pipeline are unavailable, the group model uses equal participant weights and cannot perform precision-weighted mixed-effects inference from first-level uncertainties. The group permutation test assumes participant independence and sign-symmetric errors under the null; the parametric t summary also relies on the usual one-sample model assumptions. Findings are conditional on the fixed upstream model and supplied maps.\n    26\t\n    27\t## Reproduction\n    28\t\n    29\tRun from the workspace root with the supplied Python environment:\n    30\t\n    31\t```bash\n    32\t/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/common-environment/.pixi/envs/default/bin/python analysis/group_analysis.py\n    33\t```\n    34\t\n    35\tThe script, recorded protocol, full summary, participant QC table, statistical maps, coverage mask, and figure source are included in this workspace. Software versions and the permutation seed are recorded in `results/summary.json`.\n     1\t# Group analysis\n     2\t\n     3\t`group_analysis.py` implements the group-stage analysis only. It loads the exact 54 supplied cope maps, verifies their identities against the cohort table and checksums in `input/sources.json`, checks common grids and finite values, constructs the common nonzero coverage mask, estimates the one-sample t map, and runs the positive whole-brain max-t sign-flip correction.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe inferential choices were recorded in `protocol.md`. Outputs are written to `results/` and `figures/`; maps are regenerated from the supplied data and code. An initial diagnostic attempt is preserved under `results/attempt-01-pre-maskfix/` because its p-map background encoded uncovered voxels as zero; use only the regenerated top-level outputs. The corrected p maps encode outside-mask voxels as NaN.\n     1\t# Group-analysis protocol (recorded before group inference)\n     2\t\n     3\t## Question and estimand\n     4\tAmong the complete supplied NARPS equal-indifference cohort, test whether the population mean participant gain-effect estimate is positive at any brain location, with particular interest in ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC). The estimand is the voxelwise mean of the supplied, run-averaged participant contrast parameter estimates. It is not a response slope per currency unit.\n     5\t\n     6\t## Population, unit, and inputs\n     7\tThe sampling unit is the participant. Include the 54 participant maps listed in `input/sources.json`, matched one-to-one to the pinned `equalIndifference` rows in `input/participants.tsv`; do not repeat preprocessing or individual modeling. The supplied maps are in MNI152NLin2009cAsym, 2 x 2 x 2.4 mm grid. Use all participants if file, geometry, finite-value, and coverage checks pass; otherwise document exclusions and reasons. Metadata age and gender are descriptive only, not adjustment variables.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Model and inference\n    10\tAt each voxel, use an equal-weight one-sample t model of the 54 participant effect estimates against zero. The directional hypothesis is positive. Use sign-flip permutation of participant maps under the null, with 10,000 random sign assignments and a maximum positive t statistic over a fixed whole-brain common-coverage mask; compute family-wise corrected p-values from the max-statistic distribution. The plus-one correction is used. Randomization seed: 20261016. A voxel enters the common mask only if finite and nonzero in all 54 maps; inspect the resulting spatial mask before inference. No ROI-restricted inference or post-hoc threshold is used.\n    11\t\n    12\t## vmPFC localization and reporting\n    13\tDefine vmPFC descriptively as medial (|x| <= 10 mm), anterior (y >= 25 mm), ventral (z <= 15 mm) MNI space, constrained to the common whole-brain mask. This broad coordinate rule is a transparent anatomical locator, not an independent anatomical atlas or a separate significance family. Evaluate vmPFC support only where a voxel survives whole-brain FWE correction; report peak coordinate and extent if present. Also report the unthresholded group map and distribution of estimates.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Checks and uncertainty\n    16\tCheck map IDs against metadata and sources manifest; verify image dimensions, affine, voxel sizes, finite values, and common coverage; inspect participant means, standard deviations, ranges, and outliers. Inspect the pilot before executing the full analysis. Compare the voxelwise t statistic at a selected voxel to an independent direct formula. Summarize uncertainty with group mean and 95% t interval at the selected vmPFC descriptive peak, clearly identifying this as post-hoc descriptive and not inferential evidence. Interpret a null whole-brain result as failure to find corrected evidence, not proof of no vmPFC response. Limitations include absent participant variance maps, unknown upstream estimation uncertainty, normalized regressor units, and separate gain/loss participant models.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Reproducibility\n    19\tRun from workspace root with the prepared Python 3.13.16 environment: `.../bin/python analysis/group_analysis.py`. Record software versions, seed, exact inputs, outputs, and run summary in `results/`.\n[core]\n\trepositoryformatversion = 0\n\tfilemode = true\n\tbare = false\n\tlogallrefupdates = true\n\tignorecase = true\n\tprecomposeunicode = true\n\thooksPath = /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/workspaces/narps-neutral-r1-13/.git/hooks\n[annex]\n\tuuid = 59cdfbdc-6197-424f-aff6-c3b465381ead\n\tversion = 10\n[filter \"annex\"]\n\tsmudge = git-annex smudge -- %f\n\tclean = git-annex smudge --clean -- %f\n\tprocess = git-annex filter-process\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The scientific package is unusually complete: it records a prespecified whole-brain max-statistic test, checks all 54 inputs, and reports a non-significant vmPFC result with appropriate limits. The worker-stop receipt has no committed HEAD, while the current operational Git commit is coordinator-created; I’ll keep that distinction in versioning credit. The only documented runtime path is an absolute external environment path, so I’m checking the retained mapping before deciding whether the replay prerequisites are actually available."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json\np=json.load(open(__import__('os').environ['STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET']+'/input/sources.json'))\nprint('source top keys:',list(p))\nfor k,v in p.items():\n if k\"'!='\"'files': print(k, repr(v)[:2000])\nprint('one file record:',next(iter(p['files'].items())))\nPY\ncat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.gitignore\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.entire/settings.local.json\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.codex/hooks.json\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/HEAD\"\nfind \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/refs\" -type f -maxdepth 4 -print -exec cat {} '\"\\\\;\nfind \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git\" -maxdepth 2 -type f '\"\\\\( -name '*annex*' -o -name 'config' -o -name 'description' -o -name 'packed-refs' \\\\) -print\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json\np=json.load(open(__import__('os').environ['STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET']+'/input/sources.json'))\nprint('source top keys:',list(p))\nfor k,v in p.items():\n if k\"'!='\"'files': print(k, repr(v)[:2000])\nprint('one file record:',next(iter(p['files'].items())))\nPY\ncat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.gitignore\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.entire/settings.local.json\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.codex/hooks.json\"\ncat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/HEAD\"\nfind \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/refs\" -type f -maxdepth 4 -print -exec cat {} '\"\\\\;\nfind \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git\" -maxdepth 2 -type f '\"\\\\( -name '*annex*' -o -name 'config' -o -name 'description' -o -name 'packed-refs' \\\\) -print\"","aggregated_output":"source top keys: ['study', 'collection', 'team', 'image_metadata_api', 'selection', 'maps', 'map_bytes', 'map_type', 'spatial_reference', 'upstream_processing_fixed', 'cohort_source', 'methods_source', 'checksum_scope', 'files']\nstudy 'NARPS Hypothesis 1: group inference from fixed participant effects'\ncollection 'https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/'\nteam 'AO86'\nimage_metadata_api 'https://neurovault.org/api/collections/4932/images/?limit=2000'\nselection 'All single-subject unthresholded hypo1 cope images; participant IDs exactly match the 54 equalIndifference participants in the pinned source table.'\nmaps 54\nmap_bytes 96387264\nmap_type 'participant contrast parameter estimate, not a z/t statistic or a group result'\nspatial_reference 'MNI152NLin2009cAsym'\nupstream_processing_fixed True\ncohort_source {'url': 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenNeuroDatasets/ds001734/64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268/participants.tsv', 'git_commit': '64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268', 'sha256': 'd127d700f9a519338da295ea56455bf7bb46ca572f726da72ef7b57c310687b8', 'transformation': 'Select group == equalIndifference; preserve all columns and values.'}\nmethods_source {'url': 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/poldrack/narps/0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc/ImageAnalyses/metadata_files/analysis_pipelines_for_analysis.xlsx', 'git_commit': '0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc', 'sha256': 'bfb66119c6beb26e5d3fc84638435cf6c49b40e5eb06606bfecaee0aa48d82b7', 'sheet': 'Sheet1', 'header_excel_row': 2, 'team_id': 'AO86'}\nchecksum_scope 'SHA256 values are computed from retrieved bytes; NeuroVault metadata independently supplies file sizes but no source checksums.'\none file record: ('data/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz', {'url': 'https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz', 'image_id': 118651, 'participant_id': 'sub-001', 'bytes': 1794522, 'sha256': 'baf3440fbc7f5f59dea28d231e9c5eb9cf00a15b6924342f4b79e7faf56a929a', 'transformation': 'none'})\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\ncat: /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-13-j01/operational/content/workspace/.gitignore: No such file or directory\n{\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"strategy_options\": {\n    \"checkpoint_remote\": {\n      \"provider\": \"github\",\n      \"repo\": \"leej3/stamped-in-practice\"\n    },\n    \"push_sessions\": false\n  },\n  \"absolute_git_hook_path\": true,\n  \"telemetry\": false,\n  \"checkpoints\": {\n    \"primary\": {\n      \"type\": \"git-refs\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"PostToolUse\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex post-tool-use'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex session-end'\",\n            \"timeout\": 3\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"SessionStart\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then printf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" \\\"{\\\\\\\"systemMessage\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Entire CLI is enabled but not installed or not on PATH. Installation guide: https://docs.entire.io/cli/installation#installation-methods\\\\\\\"}\\\"; exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex session-start'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"Stop\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex stop'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"SubagentStart\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex subagent-start'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"SubagentStop\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex subagent-stop'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"UserPromptSubmit\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": null,\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"sh -c 'if ! command -v entire >/dev/null 2>&1; then exit 0; fi; exec entire hooks codex user-prompt-submit'\",\n            \"timeout\": 30\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nref: refs/heads/codex/narps-neutral-r1-13\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-13-j01/operational/content/workspace/.git/refs/annex/last-index\n12311121398257a74d5f7b0e6319ba8299623a0e\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-13-j01/operational/content/workspace/.git/refs/heads/codex/narps-neutral-r1-